{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/reference/gs/state.md at exact ef555b8e5dc64bc72bf12c158cb10a5068704067 on request/submit-deadline-override, under promise git:sha1…a7ab3ab on request git:sha1…d3742a6\n\nRepublished after the second changes-requested verdict b97e92ab. All four items\nare closed, and the reviewer's own mutations are dead.\n\nG1 was mine and it was the sharpest finding of the lane. The end-to-end batch\ntest gave the in-process resident 400 ms to sign, fold and compare-and-swap act\n0 — an act that has to succeed — in a lane premised on residents being slower\nthan a fixed short wait. The reviewer made it fail five times out of five with\neighteen busy loops; I reproduced that and the deadline is now five seconds,\nwhich only the stalled act ever spends. Three runs under the same eighteen loops\npass.\n\nG2 was real and my repair had not closed it. TestEveryAppendingCommandBindsItsDeadline\nproved each command resolves its deadline, not that it keeps the context that\ncarries it, so the reviewer's mutation — resolve, discard the context, submit\nunder the old wait — stayed green through the whole package.\nTestEveryCommandRegisteringTheDeadlineBindsIt now reads the binding out of the\nsource, in the idiom internal/docset/surface.go already uses for the flags: a\nfunction that calls actFlags must assign withSubmitDeadline's context back to\nctx, and a function that binds must have registered a flag. Their exact mutation\nis red against it. A thirteenth appending command inherits the check.\n\nG3 was an ordering regression introduced by the C3 repair. main() attached and\nstarted the heartbeat before run() answered the startup refusal, so a\nmisconfigured adapter announced presence and took a lease before refusing. The\nrefusal now gates attendAs, which is the first thing that touches the workroom.\nTestAdapterRefusesBeforeAnnouncingItself holds that no room is attached and\nnothing is announced, and removing either guard turns a named test red.\n\nG4 was the reviewer's own earlier suggestion backfiring, and they said so\nplainly. Reading the variable inside newServer made cmd/gitseq-mcp and\ncmd/gitseq-github depend on the ambient GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE — the variable\nthis feature documents — so a developer with a good value set saw unrelated\ntests fail. Both packages clear it in TestMain and the tests that are about the\ndeadline set it themselves. Verified green with the variable set to 90s and to\nan unreadable value.\n\nThe branch-three wording they asked me to check was wrong in the way they\ndescribed: a file with one keyless act named that act among the other keyless\nacts. It names itself once now, and a separate case covers several.\n\nGates at this exact head: go vet ./... exit 0, go test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./...\nexit 0, 37 packages, read from the process.\n\nTwo earlier full runs reported failures and both are accounted for rather than\nwaved away. The first was TestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated\nin internal/app, at load average about five, in a package whose build graph\ncontains nothing this lane touches; it did not reproduce in isolation or under\ntwelve busy loops at either head, and it is recorded here because one\nobservation is not a verdict. The second run reported that test and\nTestSubmitRefusesAtCapacityBeforeChaining, and those were my own fault: a kill of\neighteen load generators from an earlier experiment had not taken — the subshells\nreparented and kept spinning through that run. I nearly reported them as\ncodebase flakes. The run above is the first clean-machine run since, and the\nstray processes are gone.\n\n\nRepublished after the changes-requested verdict b5e3c40c. Every item the review\nraised is closed here, and the two security defects were reproduced before being\nfixed rather than taken on the reviewer's word.\n\nS1, the batch recovery advice could cause a duplicate durable append. The\nrefusal said \"run the same file again — key X replays what landed\" whenever the\nact that timed out carried a key. Replay safety is not a property of that act: an\nact given no key lands afresh, as this package's own account of batch recovery\nsays, so a file whose first act carried no key would gain a second copy of it by\nfollowing the advice. The refusal now weighs every act at or before the failure\nand says which of two cases the author is in: the file is safe to rerun, or these\npositions carry no key and would land again. docs/reference/gs/batch.md carried\nthe same unconditional claim and is corrected in the same head.\n\nS2, the derived transport backstop could become no backstop. GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE\nof 2562047h47m15s is positive and accepted, and adding the five-second margin\nwrapped it to -2562047h47m13.709551616s, which net/http reads as no timeout at\nall — strictly worse than the fixed forty seconds it replaced. I reproduced the\narithmetic before changing anything. The addition now saturates, and\nTestBackstopOutlastsEvenAnAbsurdDeadline holds the bound at four call deadlines\nincluding the maximum.\n\nC1, the context binding was proven for one command in twelve. The reviewer made\ngs promise resolve the deadline and throw it away, and the package stayed green.\nTestEveryAppendingCommandBindsItsDeadline now asks all twelve in turn, because\nthe binding is one line per command and a missing line is silent.\n\nC2, TimedOut's Timeout() call had no red proof: the test's refusal list contained\nno net.Error at all. It now contains a refused dial as a real *net.OpError\nwrapping ECONNREFUSED, which is the case that matters — a refused dial must never\nbe read as an expiry, because nothing was appended and the author would be sent\nlooking for something that is not there.\n\nC3, the adapter repair was incomplete: the body was testable but the call in\nmain() was not, and the original mutation survived one line further up. The\nresolution moved into newServer, which every server in this package goes through,\nand the refusal it may carry is answered by run() before the adapter serves\nanything. configureResidentDeadlines is gone rather than patched.\n\nC4, the GitHub connector had no test and let a malformed value start the run and\nfail at the append. It now resolves before any observation is made, carries the\nvalue on the run's context, and two tests hold it: one that an unreadable value\nrefuses the run, and one that a stalled resident is left at this run's deadline\nrather than the default.\n\nC5, gs publish advertises --deadline and its resident read kept a ten-second\nliteral. It now reads under the same deadline, with a red proof.\n\nC6, submitDeadline guarded against a non-positive value that its only writer\nrefuses. The guard and the half-test pinning it are gone.\n\nTwo further gaps surfaced while proving the repairs, and both were mine: the\nbatch end-to-end test used a single act, so a refusal built from the failing act\nalone was indistinguishable from one built from the file — it now lands the first\nact against a real in-process resident and stalls the second; and the connector's\ndial had the same hole. Both have mutations in the table below.\n\n\nConditions met at this exact head.\n\n1. Every command that files a durable act accepts an explicit submit deadline.\nThe twelve that append — state, promise, artifact, review, review-request,\nratify, supersede, reassign-if-unclaimed, batch, merge, land and publish — take\n--deadline; the docset surface gate reads the flags out of the source, so that\nlist is the implementation's own answer rather than mine. GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE\nsupplies the value when the flag is absent, the flag wins where both are given,\nand with neither the deadline is the same ten seconds every caller had before.\n\n2. One place decides it. residentclient.ResolveSubmitDeadline is that place, and\nsubmitRequest, the GitHub connector's submit and the MCP adapter's call deadline\nall take their answer from it; no call site carries its own literal any more. A\nvalue that is not a positive duration is refused, naming what it would accept\n(\"write a positive duration, such as 30s or 2m\"), rather than being replaced by\nthe default the author was trying to escape.\n\nThe adapter needed two further changes to make that honest. Its transport\nbackstop was a fixed forty seconds, which would have cut any call deadline\nraised past it; it is now derived from the longest deadline in the policy, so\nraising the deadline is honoured rather than silently clipped. And the startup\nwiring moved out of main() into configureResidentDeadlines, because a line no\ntest can call is a line nobody has checked.\n\n3. gs batch passes the same deadline to each of its per-act submissions, and its\nrefusal on a timed-out act says what recovery needs: the act may already have\nlanded, and the key to replay under is named. An act with no idempotency_key\ncannot be replayed at all — gs state mints a random key, so a second run appends\na second copy — so that refusal says to find the act first instead. A refused act\nkeeps its own words: telling an author their act may have landed when the\nresident said it did not would be worse than saying nothing.\n\n4. Tests pin each of those. The default is ten seconds; the flag and the variable\neach raise it and the flag wins; unparsable, zero and negative values are all\nrefused; a stalled resident is left to show that the deadline reaches the dial\nrather than merely the context, at two different values; a batch act whose\ndeadline expires reports the key to replay under; and the pinned MCP deadline\ntest now reads the shared source instead of a second literal of its own.\n\n5. Eighteen mutations of the new guards, each turning a named test red, the\nwhole table in 109 seconds:\n\n  explicit value beats the environment: TestResolveSubmitDeadlinePrefersExplicitThenEnvironment\n  a value that cannot be read is refused: TestResolveSubmitDeadlineRefusesWhatItCannotUse\n  a deadline must be positive: TestResolveSubmitDeadlineRefusesWhatItCannotUse\n  an expired deadline is not a refusal: TestTimedOutSeparatesAnExpiredDeadlineFromARefusal\n  a refusal is not an expired deadline: TestTimedOutSeparatesAnExpiredDeadlineFromARefusal\n  the flag reaches the invocation: TestSubmitDeadlineComesFromTheFlagThenTheEnvironment, TestEveryAppendingCommandBindsItsDeadline\n  a refused deadline stops the command: TestEveryAppendingCommandBindsItsDeadline, TestSubmittingCommandsRefuseAnUnreadableDeadline\n  the submission dials under this invocation's deadline: TestSubmitRequestWaitsForThisInvocationsDeadline\n  the batch weighs the prefix, not one act: TestBatchSubmitRefusalWeighsTheWholePrefix\n  the batch refusal is built from the file: TestBatchReportsTheReplayKeyWhenAnActsDeadlineExpires\n  an act with no key is not called replayable: TestBatchSubmitRefusalWeighsTheWholePrefix\n  publish reads under the deadline it advertises: TestPublicationDecisionReadsUnderThisInvocationsDeadline\n  the adapter reads the environment when it is built: TestAdapterTakesItsDeadlineFromTheEnvironment\n  a refused adapter value stops the adapter: TestAdapterTakesItsDeadlineFromTheEnvironment\n  the backstop saturates rather than wrapping: TestBackstopOutlastsEvenAnAbsurdDeadline\n  the backstop outlasts the longest deadline: TestBackstopOutlastsEvenAnAbsurdDeadline, TestResidentRequestDeadlinesPreserveCallerCancellation\n  the connector refuses before observing: TestConnectorRefusesAnUnreadableDeadlineBeforeObserving\n  the connector submits under this run's deadline: TestConnectorAppendWaitsForThisRunsDeadline\n\nNothing in this table is green and nothing is excused. Three earlier entries were\nmutants that did not compile, which prove nothing about behaviour; each was\nrewritten as a mutant that builds, and two of the three then exposed real defects,\nrepaired above. Two more survived as compiling mutants because the tests around\nthem could not tell the difference — the batch end-to-end test ran a single act,\nand the connector's dial was never exercised — and both now have fixtures that\ncan.\n\n6. docs/reference/gs/ records the flag on all twelve pages that take it, with the\ndefault and the variable in the same row. gs state carries the fuller account —\nwhen to raise it, and why an expired deadline is not a refusal — and gs batch\nsays that batching does not shorten any act's wait and what its refusal will name.\ndocs/how-to/configure-an-agent.md records the variable for the adapter, which has\nno flags.\n\n7. go vet ./... clean and go test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./... green at this exact\nhead, exit 0 read from the process rather than through a pipeline, 37 packages.\n\nOne run before that reported a failure, and it is recorded rather than dropped:\nTestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated in internal/app failed\nonce with \"commit lineage unavailable\" while the machine carried a load average\nof about five. It passes twice in isolation, the package's whole build graph\ncontains nothing this lane touches (go list -deps shows no residentclient), and\nthe second complete run is the green one above. The test builds a thousand\ncommits and reads their lineage through git, so a starved subprocess fits what it\nsaid. I did not call it flaky on one observation, and it is named here so the\nnext person who sees it knows it has been seen.\n\nArchitecture: no layer contract moves. residentclient is the client-side boundary\nfor the local resident and already owned the same shape for actor identity\n(ActorEnvironment, ResolveActor); the deadline joins it as a second setting\nresolved the same way. Nothing changes what the kernel admits, what the fold\ndecides, or what any projection says, and docs/reference/architecture.md is\ntherefore not in this artifact set.","body":{"commit":"ef555b8e5dc64bc72bf12c158cb10a5068704067","path":"docs/reference/gs/state.md"}}